  "warriorsPage": {
    "title": "Restoring Hope,",
    "titleHighlight": "One Dream at a Time",
    "subtitle": "Behind every diagnosis, there is a human spirit refusing to break. Witness the power of your kindness in the lives of these brave warriors.",
    "stats": {
      "dreams": "Dreams Fulfilled",
      "investment": "Funds Raised",
      "team": "Team Members"
    },
    "readStory": "Read Story",
    "dreamFulfilled": "Dream Fulfilled ✨",
    "close": "Close story",
    "supportBtn": "Support more dreams like this",
    "featured": {
      "anetra": {
        "name": "Anetra",
        "age": "46",
        "dream": "Mental Health Retreat",
        "shortDesc": "Diagnosed with Stage 3C Triple-Negative Breast Cancer while battling heart issues.",
        "fullStory": "Already a long-term cardiac patient, she was admitted to the hospital in January 2022 for what appeared to be a heart attack. A CT scan revealed three masses, leading to a diagnosis of Stage 3C Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC). Despite managing ongoing health challenges, she has dedicated her three years of remission to advocacy. Known as the 'Big Momma' of her family, she balances caregiving for her children and grandchildren with her own intensive physical therapy.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The grant provides a necessary physical and mental 'reset.' For a caregiver and advocate who is constantly pouring into others, this staycation offers the rest required to lower her stress levels and refresh her spirit."
      },
      "janelle": {
        "name": "Janelle",
        "age": "40",
        "dream": "Attend YSC Summit",
        "shortDesc": "Living with cancer for 16 years, recently confirming spread to her brain.",
        "fullStory": "Diagnosed with Stage 2B breast cancer in 2010 at age 24, Janelle has lived with cancer for nearly 16 years. In 2012, her cancer became metastatic. Following a period of stability during which she and her husband welcomed twin boys via surrogate, she recently experienced a seizure confirming the cancer has spread to her brain.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The grant will support Janelle’s transition into advocacy. She aims to connect with the Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC) community at the summit to begin sharing her journey and providing hope to others facing similar diagnoses."
      },
      "jeanelle": {
        "name": "Jeanelle",
        "age": "37",
        "dream": "Wellness Kit for Survivors",
        "shortDesc": "Misdiagnosed for being 'too young', she battled Stage 3 TNBC. Now a yoga instructor.",
        "fullStory": "After nearly two years of being misdiagnosed because she was considered 'too young', Jeanelle was diagnosed at age 33 with Stage 3, Grade 3 Triple-Negative Breast Cancer. She endured 23 rounds of chemotherapy and immunotherapy, followed by a double mastectomy. Now serving her community as a patient advocate and yoga instructor.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The grant provides tangible tools (yoga mats, blocks) to offer free, trauma-informed wellness programs in Newark. It allows her to provide a safe space for women to manage neuropathy, chronic pain, and mental health challenges."
      },
      "susan": {
        "name": "Susan Jean",
        "age": "42",
        "dream": "Art Journal Circles",
        "shortDesc": "Professional artist who refused to let a diagnosis dim her creativity.",
        "fullStory": "A professional artist who refused to let a diagnosis dim her creativity. Diagnosed in her late 30s as a mother of a 4th grader, she faced the sudden 'curveball' of cancer at the tail end of a pandemic. Today, while she is grateful to have clear scans, she continues to manage the long-term side effects of a 10-year daily treatment plan.\n\nTHE IMPACT: With her grant, Susan hosts Art Journal Circles, gifting supplies to local communities in Las Vegas. She has made it through the metaphorical fire and is now dedicated to bringing 'buckets of water' to those still in the thick of it."
      },
      "taya": {
        "name": "Taya",
        "age": "28",
        "dream": "Trip to Harry Potter World",
        "shortDesc": "Diagnosed at 26, only ten days before her birthday. Now in remission.",
        "fullStory": "Diagnosed at just 26 years old, Taya’s life was suddenly redefined by Stage 2A Invasive Ductal Carcinoma. While pursuing her degree and working full-time, she underwent an aggressive treatment plan. The journey grew even more challenging when post-surgery pathology shifted her diagnosis to Stage 3. In October 2025, she officially entered remission.\n\nTHE IMPACT: During treatment, Taya was too ill to celebrate her son’s birthday. Now in recovery, her dream is to reclaim that lost time by taking her son to the Wizarding World of Harry Potter. This grant allows Taya to focus entirely on her son and create a lasting, happy memory with her 'living angel'."
      },
      "anonymous": {
        "name": "D.",
        "age": "44",
        "dream": "Essential Living Expenses",
        "shortDesc": "Carries a heavy family history of the disease. Currently unable to work due to treatment.",
        "fullStory": "Diagnosed with Stage 2B breast cancer, she carries a heavy family history of the disease, having lost her mother to it. Her treatment journey has been marked by severe chemotherapy-induced fatigue, which has prevented her from working and caused significant financial strain. Her primary focus remains on her recovery and being present for her daughter.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The grant provides immediate relief from the stress of meeting basic needs (food and bills). By easing the financial burden until she can safely return to work, this support allows her to focus entirely on completing her treatment and regaining her health."
      },
      "jocelyn": {
        "name": "Jocelyn",
        "age": "43",
        "dream": "Utility Arrears Assistance",
        "shortDesc": "Single mother navigating a five-year course of treatment while managing financial aftermath.",
        "fullStory": "After a lump appeared in 2020 during the pandemic lockdowns, delayed medical access resulted in the cancer growing significantly and spreading to her underarm and shoulder blade. She underwent aggressive treatment involving three types of chemotherapy, a double mastectomy, the removal of all right-side lymph nodes, radiation, and an oophorectomy. As a single mother without a support system, she is currently navigating a five-year course of chemotherapy medication while managing the long-term physical and financial aftermath of the disease.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The grant provides an essential lifeline by preventing recurring utility shut-offs. By securing her home’s electricity, the funding reduces the constant stress of basic survival, allowing her to focus on her health and her child."
      },
      "monica": {
        "name": "Monica",
        "age": "49",
        "dream": "Craft Workshops for Warriors",
        "shortDesc": "Diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 2022, she aims to turn her healing into a community resource.",
        "fullStory": "Diagnosed with ovarian cancer in September 2022 following surgery, she completed five rounds of chemotherapy between November 2022 and March 2023. This journey shifted her perspective, teaching her to prioritize her own well-being and to live with deep gratitude for her children and each new day.\n\nTHE IMPACT: The dream is to purchase supplies to host handmade craft workshops for others navigating an oncological diagnosis. The grant empowers her to turn her personal healing into a community resource, providing a creative outlet for fellow warriors and fostering connection."
      },
      "wren": {
        "name": "Wren",
        "age": "Warrior",
        "dream": "Attend the Living Beyond Breast Cancer Conference",
        "shortDesc": "Diagnosed in 2019 with stage 4 triple negative breast cancer.",
        "fullStory": "Wren was diagnosed in 2019 with stage 4 triple negative breast cancer. Through our Dream Support Program, she chose to attend the Living Beyond Breast Cancer Conference in Philadelphia, a space for learning, connection, and support among others living with metastatic cancer."
      },
      "penny": {
        "name": "Penny",
        "age": "49",
        "dream": "Trip to New York",
        "shortDesc": "Navigating reconstructive surgeries and caring for her mother, she dreams of celebrating in NYC.",
        "fullStory": "Diagnosed at 49, Penny’s battle has been grueling: three types of chemotherapy, immunotherapy, a mastectomy, and radiotherapy. The journey cost her her hair, a breast for two years, and her sense of physical peace. Despite suffering from anxiety, insomnia, and the weight of being a caregiver for her mother with dementia, Penny remains anchored by her daughter and her new 7-month-old granddaughter. She is currently on a 10-year medication plan.\n\nTHE IMPACT: Penny’s 50th birthday dream was a trip to New York, halted by radiotherapy. The €500 grant serves as a foundational contribution toward her flights and accommodation. This is the fulfillment of a life-long wish and a celebration of survival after years of putting her own needs second."
      }
    }
  },
